To prevent 404 errors in ASP.NET WebForms from redirecting away from the requested page, you can handle the `Application_Error` event in your `Global.asax` file to customize how 404 errors are handled. Here's a step-by-step guide:

1. **Configure the Web.config**:
   Ensure that your `Web.config` is set up to handle 404 errors gracefully. This is done using the `<customErrors>` element and optionally the `<httpErrors>` element.

   ```xml
   <configuration>
     <system.web>
       <customErrors mode="On" defaultRedirect="Error.aspx">
         <error statusCode="404" redirect="Error404.aspx"/>
       </customErrors>
     </system.web>

     <system.webServer>
       <httpErrors errorMode="Custom" existingResponse="Replace">
         <remove statusCode="404" subStatusCode="-1"/>
         <error statusCode="404" path="/Error404.aspx" responseMode="ExecuteURL"/>
       </httpErrors>
     </system.webServer>
   </configuration>
   ```

2. **Handle Application_Error in Global.asax**:
   In the `Global.asax` file, you can handle the `Application_Error` event to provide custom error handling logic for unhandled exceptions or 404 errors. This allows you to control the user experience when a 404 error occurs.

   ```csharp
   public class Global : System.Web.HttpApplication
   {
       protected void Application_Error(object sender, EventArgs e)
       {
           // Get the exception object
           Exception ex = Server.GetLastError();

           // Log the error (optional)
           // Log(ex);

           // Clear the error from the server
           Server.ClearError();

           // Set a custom route to handle 404 errors
           if (ex is HttpException && ((HttpException)ex).GetHttpCode() == 404)
           {
               // Redirect to a friendly 404 error page
               Response.Redirect("~/Error404.aspx");
           }
           else
           {
               // Handle other types of errors (e.g., show a generic error page)
               Response.Redirect("~/Error.aspx");
           }
       }
   }
   ```

3. **Create Custom Error Pages**:
   Create custom error pages for different scenarios. For example, create an `Error404.aspx` page to handle 404 errors and display a friendly message to users.

   ```html
   <!-- Error404.aspx -->
   <asp:Content ID="Error404Content" runat="server" ContentPlaceHolderID="MainContent">
       <h1>Page Not Found</h1>
       <p>The requested page was not found.</p>
       <a href="/">Go back to home page</a>
   </asp:Content>
   ```

